[00:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everybody keeps asking when we\'re finally getting the Flipper 1. But while everybody\'s been waiting for a mythical pocket Linux computer to sniff IP networks, something way more interesting for the actual hardware makers slipped under the radar. This is the free Welli 2. And instead of'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trying to be a mini pocket Cali machine, it went full-blown pocket electronics lab. The Flipper 1 may be built for software networks, but this thing was built to manipulate physical silicon.'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, the Free Will I 2 is an absolute beast of a device, but you\'ll notice it\'s number two. So, whatever happened to number one? Well, it turns out we\'ve been following the Free Will Eye project for years. So, let\'s hop on down to the bench and we can see the entire'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'progression of this project. Introducing the original Free Will Eye. Now, this thing is actually was one of the original Flipper Zero killers. Let me kill those lights. Hold on. This little guy is extremely extremely capable. It has GPIO on the side here, which we\'ll'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'show off in just a second. I squared C. It has UART, IR, it\'s got all your radios that you could need. It\'s a very capable little device. And interestingly enough, this and the ICS Village was the only badges that I bought at Defcon 32.'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
